\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Firm as dollar weakens further. \b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 26 (Reuter) - Asian exotics continue to maintain a firm tone as the dollar continued to be battered by the yen and mark, local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is in big bad trouble. I think the market was caught long," said the head of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "The (dollar) plunge is still fresh -- barely 36 hours. We can still see it go lower," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit opened firmer against the dollar on offshore demand, dealers in Kuala Lumpur said. \par \par \uc2  "Buying orders are mostly from Singapore and Hong Kong. Interbank dealing is thin," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said given the dollar's bearish sentiment and Malaysia's strong economic fundamentals, the ringgit could test the 2.4500 resistance level soon. \par \par \uc2  However, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Singapore bank said the market was still short on dollar/ringgit. \par \par \uc2  "The market is trying to sell dollar/Malay lower. It's going to be pretty much in a range as the market still wants to buy dollar on dips, especially around 2.4600," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was also slightly firmer against the U.S. unit in quiet trade, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was keeping the strong local dollar in check at around the 1.3900 level. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities were heard overnight in London and New York at 1.3900. This morning they are here buying dollar/Sing again," said a dealer with a European bank. \par \par \uc2  He said the Singapore dollar would continue to be bid with the U.S. unit drifting lower eventually. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also continued to firm against the dollar in early trade hovering around 24.640/650. \par \par \uc2  The midrate was fixed at 24.66. \par \par \uc2  A dealer in Singapore said there was good commercial selling of dollars for the baht. "Some of the money is also going into the Thai equity market. \par \par \uc2  The baht is expected to strenghten further against the dollar ranging between 24.60-24.70 next week, dealers in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah edged higher against the dollar in early trade on active offshore buying of rupiah from Singapore, dealers in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sentiment remaines weak, but some support is seen at around 2,227.00, said one foreign bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Another dealer said operators might cover their positions later ahead of the weekend leading to a light recovery in dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the spot rate to range between 2,226.00/2,233.00 rupiah next week. \par \par \uc2  One foreign bank dealer said the dollar would be under pressure due to month-end and tax payments factor. \par \par \uc2  "But it might be difficult to break 2,227. It's too close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lower limit which was fixed at 2,220.0 on Wednesday," he added. \par \par \uc2  Doreen Siow 65-8703092 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr5q07f3n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
