\par\par \b \uc2  Asian exotics up on U.S. dlr, but can't catch yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Hubbard \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 8 (Reuter) - Most Asian exotic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have risen sharply against the weak U.S. dollar, but in many cases they have failed to keep pace with the rising Japanese and German \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The strength of the yen and the mark means that on a trade weighted index (TWI) basis some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weakening, giving already strong economies an extra stimulus and raising the prospect of imported inflation, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar doesn't bounce back there's definitely a risk of overheating in some Asian economies," said Bernhard Eschweiler, head of emerging markets research at J.P. Morgan in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The two economies most at risk at present are Singapore and Malaysia, but Thailand, the Philippines and Indonesia may also have to adjust economic policies if the the dollar's weakness is sustained, the economists said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar soared to an all-time high against the greenback to S$1.4040/50 despite sporadic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The slide in the dollar versus the yen, coupled with a fear of Thai baht devaluation, made the Singapore dollar a safe haven in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  The fear of a Thai devaluation "caused a lot of players to cover their short Thai baht (positions) and park their funds in Sing, which has become like a safe haven for the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia the ringgit traded to a two-year high against the dollar, cracking the 2.54 ringgit level in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a high of 2.5383/93 ringgit in early Asian dealings, spurred by interbank dollar selling from both Singapore and Malaysia, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit's key upside target is currently at 2.4800 ringgit, they added. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara, which was said to be checking dollar-ringgit rates on Monday, was not seen in the market today. \par \par \uc2  In Jakarta dealers said the local market has become concerned over the effect of the weak dollar on the Indonesian economy. \par \par \uc2  They said the rupiah would probably hover around the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceiling, fixed at 2,197/2,227 rupiah on late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is still considered a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever players become jittery with the rupiah," one private bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Taipei said the plunging U.S. dollar against the Japanese yen and the mark will continue pushing the Taiwan dollar up in the near term despite expected quarter-end U.S. dollar commercial demand later this month. The Taiwan dollar soared to T$26.000 at one stage in early afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "The T$26.00 level is only a short-term resistance for the Taiwan dollar. It will climb over that level sooner or later," said one dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers did not expect major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the Taiwan dollar's appreciation as a higher Taiwan unit will help to ease domestic inflationary pressure and lower import product prices, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may buy some U.S. dollars if the Taiwan dollar rises too quickly, but just to smooth the market and not to support the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a second dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar may test the next resistance at T$25.80 in the near term, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In Bangkok dealers expect the Bank of Thailand (BOT) to continue its policy of steadying the rise of the baht against the dollar should the dollar continue to fall in overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers at both foreign and local banks in Thailand said overseas talk of a baht devaluation was "all rubbish". \par \par \uc2  The baht is expected to range in the high 24.70s and low 24.80s to the dollar over the next week. Today the baht traded 24.73-24.74. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile in Hong Kong the local dollar was steady against the greenback and little movement was expected in the near term, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong dollar has not been much affected by the fall in the U.S. unit," one said. "It should remain steady next week." \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore newsroom 65-8703080 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr3800i8d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
